<h1>why do some hires with worse grades get better jobs than me</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">They signal something the recruiter actually trusts. Grades measure how well you survived a curve while proof of work shows how well you survive the job, and employers have learned the difference, with the ones hiring for skill now the majority.</p>

<div class="tldr">
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers now use skills-based hiring</a>, up from 81% the year before, meaning most recruiters actively look past GPA.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">53% of employers have dropped degree requirements</a>, up from 30% previously, but many still screen on pedigree proxies like GPA because they lack better signals.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">Only 31% of employers who dropped degree requirements followed through with real operational change</a>; the rest still default to familiar filters.</li>
    <li><a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">Resume use fell to 67%</a>, down from 73%, as employers seek richer evidence of capability beyond self-reported credentials.</li>
    <li>The gap between worse grades and better jobs is not random: it is a demonstration gap, and the candidates who close it do so with scored, verifiable work.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>what grades actually measure (and why recruiters know it is narrow)</h2>
  <p>Grades measure performance inside a system designed for filtration, not prediction. You were tested on memorization, speed under time pressure, and compliance with assignment structures. The professor who gave you a B+ has likely never hired for the role you want. The recruiter who screens your resume knows this, even if their applicant tracking system still auto-rejects below a 3.5.</p>
  <p>The honest frame: GPA correlates weakly with job performance in most fields. It predicts who could navigate a syllabus, not who can navigate ambiguity, ship work to deadline, or persuade a cross-functional team. The candidates with worse grades who land better jobs often understood this earlier. They stopped optimizing for the filter and started building signals the filter cannot capture.</p>
  <p>This is not a motivational argument about hustle. It is structural. The time-based filter OS of university rewards a specific personality type: disciplined, rule-oriented, good at guessing what evaluators want. Many roles that pay well and grow fast reward different traits: creative problem-solving, tolerance for messy iteration, willingness to ship imperfect work and refine it live. The "worse grades" candidate may simply have spent energy on the second set of skills while the "better grades" candidate optimized for the first.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>the three signals that beat GPA in hiring decisions</h2>
  <p>Recruiters operate under uncertainty. They need evidence that reduces the risk of a bad hire. GPA is one such reduction strategy, but it is increasingly crowded out by richer signals. The candidates who outperform their grades typically show at least one of the following, and often all three.</p>
  <p><strong>Demonstrated output in the domain.</strong> A marketing candidate with a 2.8 who grew a TikTok account to 50,000 followers, documented the strategy, and can walk through the analytics beats a 3.8 who only studied marketing theory. The recruiter can ask specific questions about the work, judge the thinking, and imagine the candidate doing the job, which turns the application into proof rather than promise.</p>
  <p><strong>Referral quality and network positioning.</strong> The strongest predictor of interview conversion is still who recommends you, but the quality of the referrer matters enormously. A lukewarm internal referral from someone who barely knows you is weaker than a specific, detailed endorsement from someone who has seen your work. The worse-grades candidate may have invested in relationships with people doing the actual work, not just career center staff or alumni network figureheads.</p>
  <p><strong>Scored, comparable performance.</strong> This is where the landscape is shifting fastest. When a candidate arrives with work that has been evaluated against a professional bar, not a classroom curve, the recruiter has something concrete to compare. The score is not the credential: it is the existence of a judgment made by someone who understands the role, applied to real tasks, with feedback attached, collapsing the uncertainty that GPA was supposed to address and doing it better.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>why the demonstration gap widens after the first job</h2>
  <p>Early-career hiring is the only phase where GPA matters at all, and even there its power is fading. By the third year, no one asks. What replaces it is a track record of shipped work, and this is where the initial demonstration gap compounds.</p>
  <p>The candidate who entered with proof of work gets assigned to real projects faster, accumulates more portfolio material, and builds a narrative of increasing responsibility. The candidate who entered on GPA alone often starts in roles that value credential compliance over creative output, and the cycle reinforces. Five years out, the "worse grades" candidate who had proof is now the senior hire poachable for premium compensation. The "better grades" candidate who lacked it is still explaining what their degree was in.</p>
  <p>This is the degree-as-mortgage dynamic playing out in real time. You borrowed status upfront, but the asset depreciated because you never built equity in actual capability, while the other candidate paid as they went, in public, with work that accumulated value.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>how employers actually screen when they claim to be "skills-based"</h2>
  <p>The skills-based hiring movement is real but uneven. Most employers who dropped degree requirements did not replace them with rigorous capability assessment. They replaced them with vague "equivalent experience" language and continued to screen on whatever proxies felt familiar: school prestige, internship brand names, or yes, GPA when available.</p>
  <p>The gap between policy and practice creates an arbitrage opportunity. The candidates who win are not those who simply have "skills." They are those who make their skills legible to recruiters who are still learning how to evaluate them. A GitHub profile with no README is not legible. A portfolio with no context on what you specifically did is not legible. A list of "proficient in Python, SQL, Tableau" is not legible. What is legible: specific projects, specific decisions, specific outcomes, with enough structure that a non-technical recruiter can grasp the significance and a technical one can probe the depth.</p>
  <p>This is why scored work matters structurally. It forces the legibility. You cannot submit without producing something evaluable. The evaluation creates a common language between candidate and employer that bypasses the old proxies entirely.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>what to do if your grades are already fixed</h2>
  <p>You cannot rewrite your transcript. You can make it irrelevant faster than you think.</p>
  <p>Start with the role you want, not the credential you have. Find ten job postings. Extract the actual tasks: not "strong communication skills," but "present weekly to cross-functional stakeholders" or "own the experiment design for pricing tests." Now build one artifact that demonstrates each task. Not a course project. A real thing, with real constraints, that you can explain in detail.</p>
  <p>Submit this work for evaluation where possible. The feedback loop is the accelerator. Generic self-assessment is slow and easy to inflate. External evaluation, especially when it is specific and tied to the work you just did, tells you where you actually stand and gives you language to describe it.</p>
  <p>Finally, route around the GPA gatekeepers. Some employers still auto-filter. Others have already stopped. The <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% using skills-based hiring</a> are not evenly distributed, but they are findable. Target companies with public skills-based commitments, hiring manager posts about what they actually value, or application flows that let you submit work samples. Your energy is better spent where the game has already changed than arguing with employers still running the old filter.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>frequently asked questions</h2>
  
  <h3>Should I leave my GPA off my resume entirely?</h3>
  <p>If it is below 3.5 and you have any other signal, yes. The absence draws less attention than a number that triggers auto-rejection. Lead with what you have built.</p>
  
  <h3>Do top consulting and finance firms still care about grades?</h3>
  <p>Some do, particularly the most traditional pipelines. But even there, the trend is toward case-based interviews and work sample assessments. The firms most insistent on GPA are often the ones with the most competitive applicant pools and the least differentiated work, which is a signal about what the job actually involves.</p>
  
  <h3>How long does it take to build proof that outweighs grades?</h3>
  <p>For focused, role-specific work: weeks to a few months of deliberate project-building, not years. The key is specificity. One well-documented project that maps directly to the target role beats a year of general "learning to code" or "exploring marketing."</p>
  
  <h3>Can I recover if I am already several years out and my track record is thin?</h3>
  <p>Yes, but the strategy shifts. You need a "pivot project" that demonstrates current capability at the level you want to be hired for, plus a narrative that explains the gap without defensiveness. The project does the heavy lifting; your explanation should be brief and forward-facing.</p>
  
  <h3>Does zero score work in a way employers recognize?</h3>
  <p>zero scores submissions against professional bars, with per-submission feedback, creating a record of demonstrated capability that recruiters can review. The score is not a credential. It is evidence, and evidence is what replaces pedigree.</p>
</section>

